C('8-040',[90,91,92],'J156-8-12','7-176','Magma associé à un ensemble ordonné L','For any poset L, take point objects P_x and interval objects S_ab for a<b. Faces of S_ab are its endpoints; open refinement is equality on points, a<x<b for P_x≪°S_ab, and a′≤a<b≤b′ for S_ab≪°S_a′b′. Interior disjointness means strict comparability for two points, x≤a or b≤x for a point/interval, and b≤a′ or b′≤a for two intervals. These formulas satisfy the magasin axioms. Ordinary refinement is containment of the closed endpoint intervals. A literal set-figure realization with nonempty cells requires density, and orthogonality agrees with set disjointness only under further order conditions.','proof sketched','posetintervals')
C('8-041',[93],'J156-8-12','180-235','Mag L1 ... divisible ... Pour que Mag L2 ...','For the poset-interval magasin, L1 is precisely order density: every a<b has a<x<b. L2 is asserted equivalent to density plus: if x is strictly comparable to every y∈(a,b), then x≤a or x≥b. The text further reformulates this by intersections of principal lower/upper sets; that last reformulation has weakly justified quantifiers and needs checking when elements are maximal but not greatest.','proof sketched','posetintervals',confidence='medium')
C('8-042',[94],'J156-8-12','270-299','M est fidèle sss L est totalement ordonné','The poset-interval magasin has full-shadow faithful disjointness iff L is a chain. Necessity follows from incomparable points; sufficiency uses the common subinterval in each overlap ordering. It is point-like iff L is also dense. This is about this magasin including point objects, not the later proper-quotient orthogonality space with points omitted.','proved there','posetintervals')
C('8-043',[95,96,97,98,99,100],'J156-8-12','302-434','Drap(Drap*(L)) → Fig fin(M) ... bijective','A finite compatible collection is exactly a selection of interstitial points and consecutive intervals of one finite chain of endpoints. Finite figures are uniquely disjoint unions of canonical connected figures F_T for a linearly ordered family of nonempty finite flags T. Equivalently, choose a vertex flag D and any subset of its n−1 adjacent edges; for n≥1 this gives 2^(n−1) figures with that vertex set. The connected-component step is explicitly left to verify in the proof indication on scan99.','proof sketched','posetintervals','Enumerated in computations.py for n=1,…,6, obtaining 1,2,4,8,16,32; empty D gives one empty figure separately.')
C('8-044',[100,101,102,112],'J156-8-13','94-200','Hypothèse H ... supp(X)=Omb(X)','Assume H: every minimal element of L is a least element, and dually every maximal element is greatest. Then the support of the full elementary figure at X is its refinement shadow; interior support is its open-refinement shadow. Explicitly supp°(P_a)={P_a}; supp°(S_ab) consists of P_x with a<x<b and intervals S_xy with a≤x<y≤b. Adding selected endpoints adds exactly those point objects. The proof uses outside intervals S_xa and S_by and handles extremal cases.','proved there','posetintervals')
C('8-045',[103],'J156-8-13','203-227','cosupp°({Pa})={Pb}; supp°({Pa})≠{Pa}','For L={a,b,c}, a<b and c<b, the printed cosupp°(P_a)={P_b} and ensuing nonsingleton supp°(P_a) are false under the earlier formulas: S_ab is also perpendicular to P_a, and the true double perpendicular is {P_a}. The independent displayed computation supp°({P_a,P_b,S_ab})=M is correct and does show the whole-support identity can fail without H. The margin may read Cancelé rather than Calculé; no ink decision is made.','false as stated (with the counterexample, every hypothesis checked)','posetintervals','All five elements, incidence, refinement and orthogonality tables recomputed exhaustively in computations.py; corrected statement C-SA21-8-045R.',confidence='high')
C('8-045R',[103],'J156-8-13','203-227','Refined computation by this run, not substituted into the source','In the same five-element V-poset magasin: cosupp°(P_a)={P_b,S_ab}; supp°(P_a)={P_a}; cosupp°(whole S_ab)=∅ and supp°(whole S_ab)=M. The whole-figure counterexample survives while the singleton counterexample does not.','proved in this run; reconstruction','posetintervals','Exhaustive finite certificate in computations.py and computations_output.txt.',confidence='high')
C('8-046',[104,105,106,107,108,109,110,111],'J156-8-13','264-384','préfigures connexes ... (T,α)','Finite prefigures are finite subsets of figures. Their connected components have the form F_T∖α, T a nonempty finite flag, α a subset of its two endpoints (empty for a singleton). Components are ordered by position with a stricter separation rule at a common missing endpoint. Closedness means no endpoints omitted. The stated openness classification assumes H and removes endpoints except global extrema; a nonempty singleton poset is an exceptional degenerate case needing separate treatment.','proof sketched','posetintervals')
C('8-047',[111,113,114,115],'J156-8-14','240-366','supp°(Φ∪Ψ)=supp°Φ∪supp°Ψ','For finite prefigures separated in positional order by a point or interval X, double-perpendicular support of their union is the disjoint union of their supports. Induction gives additivity over combinatorial connected components. The proof specifically uses that Φ,Ψ are prefigures; the margin adds this hypothesis. The author states H was not used.','proved there','posetintervals')
C('8-048',[115],'J156-8-15','56-75','Lemme de composition des segments','For a<b<c, the prefigure {S_ab,P_b,S_bc} and S_ac have identical perpendicular sets, hence equal double-perpendicular supports. This segment-composition lemma does not require H. It removes a present degree-two vertex without changing support.','proved there','posetintervals')
C('8-049',[113,116,117,126],'J156-8-15','80-137','Tout support constructible ... préfigure irrédondante','Under H and finite-figure constructibility, every constructible support has a unique irredundant prefigure representative, obtained by composing adjacent intervals across present degree-two vertices. Its support is the disjoint union of open-refinement shadows of its intervals and its included point objects. Maximal interval elements and isolated/refined endpoints recover the representative. The H dependency must be carried from scans100,112 and explicit reminder126.','proved there','posetintervals')
C('8-050',[118],'J156-8-15','142-149','À vérifier ! Facile ... plus petit support constructible fermé','Under the standing H and finite constructibility setting, the least closed constructible support containing supp°Φ is asserted to be supp°(closure of Φ). The author marks the assertion À vérifier and appends a proposed expression via face saturation; this is a flagged proof obligation, not a completed proof on these pages.','open on the pages','posetintervals')
C('8-051',[105,106,119,120],'J156-8-15','190-298','cinq types combinatoires','Vertices of a finite prefigure have degree 0,1,2. Degree-zero vertices in its face closure are present isolated vertices; degree-one vertices are included/excluded boundary vertices; degree-two vertices are included redundant or excluded lacunary vertices. The example with intervals ab,bc,cd and point vertices a,c,e realizes all five types: e isolated, a proper boundary, d improper boundary, c redundant, b lacunary. Terminology changes: S_ab is now an interval, and its full face figure a segment.','proved there','graphs','The finite degree/component and boundary examples are recomputed in computations.py; the earlier componentwise boundary convention differs from the final degree-one definition.')
C('8-052',[121,122,123,124,125,126,127],'J156-8-16','6-351','Une préfigure quelconque est déterminée ... Cosupp°','Finite prefigures are parametrized either by disjoint closed interval components Ψ, a vertex flag Δ containing their endpoints, included endpoints and selected lacunary internal vertices, or simply by Δ together with selected point/adjacent-interval subsets whose faces cover Δ. Irredundancy means every internal vertex is lacunary. Under H, for nonempty Δ with endpoints a,b, the perpendicular set is the disjoint union of open shadows of the complement prefigure inside F_Δ and positional regions M_<a,M_>b. The final empty case is excluded explicitly.','proof sketched','posetintervals')
